.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CHANGE-PASSWORD 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-change-password \- change your own Euclid password, or reset another user's
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eam change-password
[\fB\-\-user\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIUSERID\fR]
[\fB\-\-old\-password\fR|\fB\-o\fR \fIPASSWORD\fR]
[\fB\-\-new\-password\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fIPASSWORD\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Replaces a password via the eam service's
.I change-password
action. Two things wear that one name, and which of them a command is
follows from
.BR \-\-user :
.TP
.B Changing your own
.B \-\-user
omitted, or naming yourself. The current password must be given as well,
which is what proves the change may be made \- so a session somebody
walked away from, or a token that leaked, cannot be turned into the
account itself. Self-service; no administrator privileges required.
.TP
.B Resetting somebody else's
.B \-\-user
naming another user. No old password is given, because an administrator
is not supposed to know one; being an administrator is the proof
instead. Requires administrator privileges.
.PP
A password left off the command line is asked for at the terminal
without being echoed, and a new one asked for that way is asked for
twice, since a typo in something nobody can see would otherwise only
surface at the next login. A command line, by contrast, is visible in
the shell history and, while the command runs, in the process list \- so
the options exist mainly for where there is no terminal to ask at, such
as under a scheduler, and using one prints a note saying so.
.PP
Nothing is printed on success.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
User whose password to reset. Omit for your own, which is the only one
that can be changed without administrator privileges. Naming yourself is
the same as omitting it, old password and all.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-old\-password " " \fIPASSWORD\fR
Your current password. Asked for at the terminal if omitted. Rejected
together with a
.B \-\-user
naming somebody else, which takes no old password.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new\-password " " \fIPASSWORD\fR
The new password. Asked for twice at the terminal if omitted.
.SH SESSIONS
Changing a password does not end any session, including the caller's
own. The bearer token
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
stored is a signed JWT, verified against the server's signing secret
rather than against anything the password change rewrites, so it stays
valid until it expires (one hour by default). The new password is what
the next
.B login
wants.
.PP
Access keys are likewise untouched: they are a separate credential, and
are managed with
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-access-key (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-access-key (1).
.SH PERMISSIONS
The action is gated like any other, on the
.I eam:change-password
permission. Members of the
.I administrator
user group pass the gate outright; for anybody else it has to be granted
by a role, which no built-in role except
.I account-administrator
holds \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1).
.PP
Users that do not log in with a password at all \- one provisioned from
an OpenID Connect or SAML identity provider, or an application's
technical principal \- are refused
(\fIHTTP 409\fR). Those accounts are deliberately created without a
password, and giving one would be a way in that they were made not to
have.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The password was changed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the two new passwords did not match, the
request could not be sent, the old password was wrong, the caller is not
an administrator and named somebody else, the named user does not exist
or does not use a password, or the server otherwise rejected the
request. If the failure was an expired or invalid session (\fIHTTP
401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying. A wrong old password is
.I not
that failure and leaves the session alone.
.SH EXAMPLES
Change your own, asked for at the terminal:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam change-password
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reset another user's password as an administrator:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam change-password \e
    --user jdoe
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactively, from a script that has no terminal to be asked at:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam change-password \e
    --old-password "$OLD" --new-password "$NEW"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-register (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-user (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-access-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1)
